{"step_index":39,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T04:50:08Z","input_tokens":9335,"cache_read_tokens":149261,"output_tokens":1876,"thinking":"The code contains a critical bug in how `box()` objects and `V5_Start` are handled within `v5_env.py` and `v5_organize.py`. This leads to improper processing. Fixing this error is paramount.\n\n\nThe `box()` function's behavior in `v5_env.py` and `v5_organize.py` is suspect because object matrices aren't immediately updated after location/rotation changes within the Blender Python environment. The `ob.matrix_world` remains the identity matrix after setting the location and rotation. This implies an issue with how the object's bounding box is calculated. The default location of a newly created mesh object is likely at the origin (0, 0, 0). Examining the code at line 1065 of `v5_env.py` is needed to verify this.\n\n\nThe bounding box calculation `_bb` is incorrect because `_ob.matrix_world` remains the identity matrix after transformations, meaning all `box()` objects' locations are being overwritten with `(0, 0, 0)`. This occurs because of the flawed bounding box calculation using the incorrect matrix. The impact is every `box()`'s location is reset to the origin, which explains why the ads that had different z rotations were in the same `dims` bucket.\n\n\nEvery object's world location is reset to (0, 0, 0) after transformation. This affects numerous objects, including the brake boards, hangars, marshal posts, big screens, bridge piers, gantry components, pit garage, and grandstand elements. The bounding box calculations are demonstrably flawed, leading to the misplacement of these objects. These objects' `dims` buckets are incorrectly assigned due to the reset location.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"$b = \"D:\\APPs\\steam\\steamapps\\common\\Blender\\blender.exe\"\n$py = @'\nimport bpy\nfrom mathutils import Vector\n\nbpy.ops.wm.open_mainfile(filepath=r\"D:\\项目session\\blender-supercar\\LATEST\\v5_stage_anim.blend\")\nat_origin = []\ntrack = bpy.data.objects[\"V5_Track\"]\nprint(\"V5_Track location:\", tuple(round(x, 1) for x in track.location))\n\nfor ob in bpy.data.objects:\n    if ob.type == 'MESH' and ob.name.startswith(\"V5_\"):\n        bb = [ob.matrix_world @ Vector(c) for c in ob.bound_box]\n        c = Vector((sum(v.x for v in bb)/8, sum(v.y for v in bb)/8, sum(v.z for v in bb)/8))\n        if c.length < 1.0:\n            at_origin.append(ob.name)\n\nprint(f\"V5_ objects piled at (0,0,0): {len(at_origin)} out of {sum(1 for o in bpy.data.objects if o.type=='MESH' and o.name.startswith('V5_'))}\")\nprint(\"Sample of objects piled at (0,0,0):\", sorted(at_origin)[:40])\n'@\n$tmp = \"C:\\Users\\86052\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\16b5f3cb-4bce-43d1-a09a-ebf98bd665b7\\scratch\\check_origin_pile.py\"\nSet-Content -Path $tmp -Value $py -Encoding UTF8\n& $b -b --factory-startup -P $tmp","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolSummary":"Verify V5 objects piled up at world origin"}}]}
